==== Upgrade from ZyXEL customized OpenWrt ====

=== Architecture and kernel size change from AR71xx to ATH79 notes ===

**Do not use Firmware OpenWrt Install unless you have modified the settings in the U-Boot loader**

The ZyXEL factory firmware is based on the AR71xx architecture and has an older kernel, therefore the installation to 22.x (ATH79) needs to be performed in two steps, otherwise the device will end up in a boot loop and needs to be recovered with the factory software using tftp. Reason being the 22.x kernel is bigger and needs to be loaded in two steps.

Firstly, install an AR71xx version, 19.07.10 [[https://archive.openwrt.org/releases/19.07.10/targets/ar71xx/generic/openwrt-19.07.10-ar71xx-generic-NBG6616-squashfs-factory.bin]]

After the successful installation of 19.07.10 - you can connect to the router with ssh

<code>
ssh root@192.168.1.1
</code>

This will allow you to access the U-Boot boot loader settings from within the ssh shell. (Doing this change from factory firmware is not possible)

<code>
fw_setenv boot_openwrt bootm 0x9F120000
fw_setenv bootcmd run boot_openwrt
</code>

After the settings are done, a sysupgrade can be performed to install the 22.x (ATH79) based OpenWrt.


<code>
sysupgrade -F -n -v /tmp/openwrt-22.03.2-ath79-generic-zyxel_nbg6616-squashfs-sysupgrade.bin
</code>

Reference: [[https://forum.openwrt.org/t/upgrade-zyxel-nbg6616-from-19-07-10-to-22-03-2-ar71xx-to-ath79/140486]]


=== Upgrade / installation procedure ===


On the ZyXEL firmware there is one large mtd partition for the kernel and everything. SSH into the router, user "root" and password (default is "1234"), and write the sysupgrade image to /dev/mtd6:

<code>
# mtd -r write /tmp/firmware.bin /dev/mtd6
</code>

The following is a more detailed example of installing OpenWrt.

<code>
ssh root@192.168.1.254
cd /tmp/
# the prompt should show root@nbg6161:/tmp
wget http://downloads.openwrt.org/releases/18.06.2/targets/ar71xx/generic/openwrt-18.06.2-ar71xx-generic-NBG6616-squashfs-sysupgrade.bin -O firmware.bin
mtd -r write /tmp/firmware.bin /dev/mtd6
Unlocking /dev/mtd6 ...
Writing from /tmp/firmware.bin to /dev/mtd6 ...     
Rebooting ... [w]
</code>

Now you can configure the device via ''%%http://192.168.1.1/%%''

